  "articleBody": "It's super exciting to see how AI can help law firms, but there are some pitfalls, especially when it comes to client confidentiality. You're dealing with sensitive information, and a single mistake can have big consequences. It’s not just about what you disclose; it's about *how* AI tools handle the data you feed them. Being proactive about managing these risks is a non-negotiable part of responsible AI adoption. Let's make sure your firm avoids the common missteps that can compromise client trust and regulatory compliance.\n\nFirst, a huge no-no is feeding confidential client information into public or consumer-grade AI tools. These platforms often retain data for model training, effectively making your client's sensitive details part of their public knowledge base. Second, never rely on AI-generated legal research or citations without thorough verification by a human lawyer. We've all heard stories of 'hallucinations' – AI making things up – and in law, that's just unacceptable. Third, remember to assign a clear, responsible lawyer for every AI initiative within the firm. Accountability is paramount. Fourth, your inventory of AI tools shouldn't just focus on the obvious; embedded software features and non-lawyer staff using AI for tasks also need to be included in your audit. Finally, don't treat a one-time AI review as a permanent solution. Models, vendors, and workflows change constantly, so your oversight needs to be ongoing. Catching these issues early helps protect your firm from significant risks.\n\nWhat to Expect During the Process:\nDuring an AI audit, expect a deep dive into your firm's technology, workflows, and vendor agreements. We'll inventory all AI tools, assess data flows, review technical controls, and test outputs. The process aims to identify risks, prioritize remediation, and establish ongoing governance, often following frameworks like NIST’s AI Risk Management Framework to ensure thoroughness.\n\nConcerned about your firm's AI confidentiality? Reach out, and let's secure your practice against these common mistakes.",
